<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kayseri</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caesarea Mazaca, Mazaca</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kayseri is a city in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, at the foot of the extinct volcano Erciyes. As Caesarea Mazaca it was the capital of the kingdom of Cappadocia and the see of Basil the Great. Taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danishmendids">Danishmendids</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> of Rum, it gained many mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, and it remains a major commercial centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayseri.jpg" alt="Kayseri" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carole Raddato from FRANKFURT, Germany · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_Erciyes_(Argaeus),_Turkey_(31870998063).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
